      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This study evaluates the safety and efficacy of the XIENCE V 4.0 mm stent for the treatment of de novo native coronary artery lesions."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In the SPIRIT III trial, the XIENCE V everolimus-eluting stent (EES), compared with the TAXUS EXPRESS(2) paclitaxel-eluting stent (PES) in 2.5-3.75 mm diameter coronary arteries, resulted in reduced angiographic late loss (LL), noninferior rates of target vessel failure (TVF), and fewer major adverse cardiac events (MACE)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "The SPIRIT III 4.0 mm registry was a concurrent arm of the SPIRIT III trial consisting of 69 nonrandomized patients with lesions <or=28 mm in length and reference vessel diameter 3.75-4.25 mm treated with a 4.0 mm EES. The primary endpoint was 8-month in-segment LL compared with the randomized PES arm."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In-segment LL was 0.17 +/- 0.38 mm in the 4.0 mm EES registry compared with 0.28 +/- 0.48 mm in the PES arm (P < 0.0001 for noninferiority). The 1-year rates of ischemia-driven TVF (cardiac death, myocardial infarction [MI], or target vessel revascularization) and MACE (cardiac death, MI, or target lesion revascularization [TLR]) were numerically, but not statistically, lower in the 4.0 mm EES patients compared with the randomized PES patients (5.9 vs. 11.3%, P = 0.27 and 5.9 vs. 10.3%, P = 0.36, respectively). There was no difference in 8-month LL or 1-year TVF or MACE between the 4.0 mm EES and randomized EES patients."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "In large coronary arteries, the 4.0 mm EES results in low rates of LL at 8 months and adverse clinical events at 1 year."
        }
      ]},
